WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.040
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:03.520
It’s Thanksgiving week here in the United
States, so today I thought I would share with

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.180
you a story of gratitude.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.380
So several months ago, Sarah got me tickets
to see the musical Hamilton in New York City,

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.860
and I should say that Sarah and I are both huge
fans of Hamilton.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.170
Like, we’ve listened to the cast recording
hundreds of times, and now we were gonna have

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:19.150
this once-in-a-lifetime opportunity to see
the show with the original cast, and we were

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:22.400
gonna go to some art galleries and visit all
our favorite places from back when we were

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.000
first married and lived in New York.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.640
So we got to the airport a little early, and
hurray our flight was on time, so we settled

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.440
in to an airport bar for some snacks, and
we were debating whether I should direct message

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.950
Lin Manuel Miranda, who, not to brag, follows
me on Twitter, and generally just feeling

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.140
sort of giddy, and then I took a bite of my
sandwich and more or less immediately knew

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.000
that we were not going to see Hamilton after
all.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.630
A bit of relevant background: I have this
weird disease called eosinophilic esophagitis.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:50.280
Basically, I have this mild-ish inflammation
of my esophagus, which usually is not a problem,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.560
but then very occasionally, food will get
stuck part of the way down my esophagus, and

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.480
that’s a real bummer because when it happens,
I can’t even successfully swallow my own

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.180
spit, so I just sort of throw up every two
or three minutes for several hours until a

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.000
doctor sticks a tube down my throat and clears
the blockage.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.860
So okay, I can’t even peel away all the
layers of luck and privilege that led to this

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.890
moment of misfortune in the Indianapolis airport:
the ability to afford a broadway show and

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.980
a trip to New York city, the joy of a spouse
who loves you enough to get you Hamilton tickets,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.640
access to excellent medical care so your esophagial
blockage is an inconvenience rather than a

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.660
catastrophe. The list goes on and on.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.500
But still, I was pretty bummed out when we
had to leave the airport and go to the hospital.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.820
Now of course it wasn’t hard to find a happy
home for our Hamilton tickets, and the airline

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.780
(thank you, Delta) was amazingly cool about
refunding our tickets.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.530
But yeah, I was still in a pretty foul mood,
partly because I was vomiting every two minutes,

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.320
and partly because I don’t love being reminded
that what I think of as myself is in fact

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:44.420
wholly dependent upon an unreliable and unpredictable
machine called my body.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.259
Okay, so after several hours at the hospital,
a woman named Carmen came by to transport

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:50.759
my hospital bed and me to the endoscopy room.

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:53.369
Along the way, she asked if I had kids, and
I said “yeah,” she said, “It’s nice

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:55.990
that your wife can stay with you as you go
through this,” and I was like, “Well,

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.619
we’re supposed to be on vacation together!”

00:01:57.619 --> 00:02:00.559
And then she said, “Maybe after you guys
get home tonight, you can watch a movie together.”

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.720
And I was like, “Maybe.”

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.660
And then she said, “So you’re married
and you have kids.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.820
Do you like your job?”

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:05.800
And I said, “Yeah.”

00:02:05.809 --> 00:02:08.589
And then she says, “Well, you’ve got the
basics covered.”

00:02:08.589 --> 00:02:12.000
And then I was in the OR, and then I was unconscious,
and then I could swallow again, and I woke

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.440
up thinking, “Well, I’ve got the basics
covered.”

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.340
That night, Sarah and I went home and watched
a movie.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.800
It was a pretty good movie, actually.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.460
Since then, I’ve thought about that day
and Carmen many times.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.040
I love my friends and family, and I am loved
by them.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.319
I’ve got the basics covered.

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:26.950
Okay, so flash forward six months to last
week.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.420
We’ve just started filming our new health
and fitness show 100 Days.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:33.510
Starting January first, you can watch my best
friend Chris and I try to have a healthy midlife

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.880
crisis over at youtube.com/100days.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.720
Anyway, as part of the show, we have to exercise
six days a week, often at very odd hours because

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.760
of our work schedules.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.260
So last Wednesday, I had to wake up at 5:10
in the morning to go to a spin class, and

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.751
I was not psyched about it.

00:02:46.751 --> 00:02:49.500
So I walk into this spin class, and there’s
Carmen.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.260
And she says, “Hey, John, remember me?”

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.380
And I say, “Yeah.”

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.400
And she says, “I’m your spin instructor.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.940
This is what I do before work.”

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.660
And of course, it turned out to be an excellent
spin class.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.170
So, this Thanksgiving, I am grateful to Carmen.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:05.310
When you are kind to strangers, you never
know what it’s gonna mean to them, but sometimes

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.770
it means a great deal.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:07.970
So thank you, Carmen.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.280
Happy Thanksgiving.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:10.410
End screen!

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:14.040
Here you’ll find some old Thanksgivingsy
vlogbrothers videos, as well as a self-care

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:18.030
bunny from Rosianna who is here to remind
you that Carmen thinks you’re awesome, and

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.250
she knows from awesome.

